{
  "Answer": {
    "Title": "Workspace missing app paths causes deploy failure",
    "Explanation": [
      "The workflow shows build-linux, build-mac and build-windows all finished with SUCCESS, so the builds are green. Each build creates its app file (for example app/linux/app.linux), but persist_to_workspace only lists meta/\u003cplatform\u003e. That means only the metadata directories go into the workspace.",
      "",
      "The deploy job attaches the workspace at '.', and its find output lists only ./meta/{linux,mac,windows}/build.txt. The first check, `test -f app/linux/app.linux`, therefore fails, and the step exits with code 1 under bash -eo pipefail. As a result, deploy and the whole workflow show FAILED. The store_artifacts step still reports SUCCESS, but the app directory does not exist, so it uploads nothing useful.",
      "",
      "The fix is to persist the app paths in each build job: add app/linux, app/windows and app/mac (alongside meta/\u003cplatform\u003e) to the respective persist_to_workspace paths, with root '.'. Attach at '.' then recreates app/\u003cplatform\u003e/app.\u003cplatform\u003e, so the verify step passes and store_artifacts uploads all three apps.",
      "",
      "The other options do not satisfy the requirement:",
      "- Storing artifacts on the builds only does not put the apps in the workspace.",
      "- Fixing one platform only still fails the other two checks.",
      "- Changing the attach location does not help, because the files were never persisted.",
      "- Removing the verify step only hides the problem and uploads nothing."
    ],
    "ActualAnswer": [
      "{",
      "  \"builds_green\": true,",
      "  \"deploy_green\": false,",
      "  \"fix\": \"persist_app_paths\",",
